⟦200⟧results, relating to strong as well as weak variations, are obtained by a method which permits of the expression of the variation of an integral as a line integral taken along the varied curve. Let A, B be the end points, and let the stationary curve AB be drawn. If the end points A, B are not a pair of conjugate points, and if the point conjugate to A does not lie on the arc AB, then we may find a point A′, on the backward continuation of the stationary curve BA beyond A, so near to A that the point conjugate to A′ lies on the forward continuation of the arc AB beyond B. This being the case, it is possible to delimit an area of finite breadth, so that the arc AB of the stationary curve joining A, B lies entirely within the area, and
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Field of stationary curves.}}
no two stationary curves drawn through A′ intersect within the area. Through any point of such an area it is possible to draw one, and only one, stationary curve which passes through A′. This family of stationary curves is said to constitute a ''field'' of stationary curves about the curve AB. We suppose that such a field exists, and that the varied curve AQPB lies entirely within the delimited area. The variation of the integral <math>\int \text{F}(x,\, y,\, y')dx</math> is identical with the line integral of F taken round a contour consisting of the varied curve AQPB and the stationary curve AB, in the sense AQPBA. The line integral may, as usual, be replaced by the sum of line integrals taken round a series of cells, the external boundaries of the set of cells being identical with the
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{sc|Fig.}} 4.}}
given contour, and the internal boundaries of adjacent cells being traversed twice in opposite senses. We may choose a suitable set of cells as follows. Let Q, P be points on the varied curve, and let A′Q, A′P be the stationary curves of the field which pass through Q, P. Let P follow Q in the sense
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|General expression for variation of an integral.}}
AQPB in which the varied curve is described. Then the contour consisting of the stationary curve A′Q, from A′ to Q, the varied curve QP, from Q to P, and the stationary curve A′P, from P to A′, is the boundary of a cell (fig. 4). Let us denote the integral of F taken along a stationary curve by round brackets, thus (A′Q), and the integral of F taken along any other curve by square brackets, thus [PQ]. If the varied curve is divided into a number of arcs such as QP we have the result
{{block center|<math>[\text{AQPB}]-(\text{AB}) = \Sigma\{(\text{A}'\text{Q})+[\text{QP}]-(\text{A}'\text{P})\},</math>}}
and the right-hand member can be expressed as a line integral taken along the varied curve AQPB.

⟦1700⟧To effect this transformation we seek an approximate expression
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{sc|Fig.}} 5.}}
for the term <math>(\text{A}'\text{Q})+[\text{QP}]-(\text{A}'\text{P})</math> when Q, P are near together. Let <math>\Delta s</math> denote the arc QP, and <math>\psi</math> the angle which the tangent at P to the varied curve, in the sense from A to B, makes with the axis of <math>x</math> (fig. 5). Also let <math>\phi</math> be the angle which the tangent at P to the stationary curve A′P, in the sense from A′ to P, makes with the axis of <math>x</math>. We evaluate <math>(\text{A}'\text{Q})-(\text{A}'\text{P})</math> approximately by means of a result which we obtained in connexion with the problem of variable limits. Observing that the angle here denoted by <math>\psi</math> is equivalent to the angle formerly denoted by <math>\pi+\omega</math> (cf. fig. 1), while <math>\tan\phi</math> is equivalent to the quantity formerly denoted by <math>y'</math>, we obtain the approximate equation
{{block center|<math>(\text{A}'\text{Q})-(\text{A}'\text{P}) = -\Delta s.\cos\psi\left\{\text{F}(x,y,p)+(\tan\psi-p)\frac{\partial\text{F}}{\partial p}\right\}_{p=\tan\phi}</math>}}
which is correct to the first order in <math>\Delta s</math>. Also we have
{{block center|<math>[\text{QP}] = \Delta s\, .\, \cos\psi\text{F}(x,y,\, \tan\psi)</math>}}
correctly to the same order. Hence we find that, correctly to the first order in <math>\Delta s</math>,
{{block center|<math>(\text{A}'\text{Q})+[\text{QP}]-(\text{A}'\text{P}) = \text{E}(x,y,\, \tan\phi,\, \tan\psi)\Delta s,</math>}}
where
{{block center|<math>\text{E}(x,\, y,\, \tan\phi,\, \tan\psi)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>=\cos\psi\left\{\text{F}(x,y,\, \tan\psi)-\text{F}(x,y,p)-(\tan\psi-p)\frac{\partial\text{F}}{\partial p}\right\}_{p=\tan\phi}.</math>}}
When the parametric method is used the function E takes the form
{{block center|<math>\left(\lambda\frac{\partial f}{\partial\dot{x}}+\mu\frac{\partial f}{\partial\dot{y}}\right)_{\dot{x}=\lambda,\, \dot{y}=\mu}-\left(\lambda\frac{\partial f}{\partial\dot{x}}+\mu\frac{\partial f}{\partial\dot{y}}\right)_{\dot{x}=l,\, \dot{y}=m}</math>}}
where <math>\lambda</math>, <math>\mu</math> are the direction cosines of the tangent at P to the curve AQPB, in the sense from A to B, and <math>l</math>, <math>m</math> are the direction cosines of the tangent at P to the stationary curve A′P, in the sense from A′ to P.

⟦2900⟧The function E, here introduced, has been called Weierstrass's ''excess function''. We learn that the variation of the integral, that
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Weierstrass's excess function.}}
is to say, the excess of the integral of F taken along the varied curve above the integral of F taken along the original curve, is expressible as the line integral <math>\int\text{E}ds</math> taken along the varied curve. We can therefore state a ''sufficient'' (but not necessary) condition for the existence of an extremum in the form:—When the integral is taken along a

